# 11. MENTAL HEALTH SUPPORT — A HUMAN-CENTERED FEATURE

This is one of the most important and sensitive parts of CampusX.

The experience must feel calm, respectful, private, and supportive.

Do not use aggressive neon effects or overwhelming visuals here.

## Mental Health Landing Page

Create:
- Calm visual identity.
- Welcoming message.
- Privacy information.
- Supportive introduction.
- AI emotional support entry point.
- Direct university psychologist contact.
- Request support option.

Example:

"You don't have to figure everything out alone."

"Your well-being matters."

## AI Emotional Support

Create a separate conversational experience.

The assistant should:
- Listen without judgment.
- Respond with empathy.
- Avoid diagnosing users.
- Avoid pretending to replace a psychologist.
- Encourage professional support when appropriate.

## High-Risk Detection and Escalation

The system must recognize potential indicators of self-harm or suicidal thoughts.

When high-risk indicators appear:

1. Stop treating the conversation as an ordinary chatbot interaction.
2. Display a supportive and urgent message.
3. Encourage immediate contact with qualified human support.
4. Provide direct access to the university psychologist.
5. Provide appropriate emergency guidance.
6. Route a support request to the authorized university support team only according to approved policy and consent rules.

Do not expose private mental health conversations to ordinary administrators.

Use strict role-based access and data protection.

Do not claim absolute confidentiality if escalation or data access may occur. Explain privacy limits clearly.

This feature supports students; it does not replace professional medical or psychological care.

